WEBVTT

00:00:11.890 --> 00:00:23.080
<v Ira Glass>His brother needed somebody last minute, because a guy bailed on him. Jeff also had somebody bail on him. He'd moved back home for the summer, for this girl he liked.
<v Ira Glass>他兄弟临时需要人手，因为有个家伙放了他鸽子。杰夫也被人放过鸽子。他夏天刚搬回家，为了一个他喜欢的女孩。

00:00:23.080 --> 00:00:28.460
<v Jeff>And it became, like, really apparent right away that that wasn't going to work out.
<v Jeff>很快就很明显，那段感情是不会有结果的。

00:00:28.460 --> 00:00:37.120
<v Ira Glass>So he had some time on his hands. His brother was doing a research study on black bears. His brother was a wildlife biologist and offered him this job as a field tech.
<v Ira Glass>所以他闲了下来。他兄弟正在做一项关于黑熊的研究。他兄弟是位野生动物生物学家，向他提供了一份外勤技术员的工作。

00:00:37.120 --> 00:00:41.530
<v Jeff>And a summer working with black bears was a dream, so--
<v Jeff>能过一个研究黑熊的暑假简直是梦想，所以——

00:00:41.530 --> 00:00:45.210
<v Ira Glass>That wouldn't be a dream for everybody. Why was that a dream for you?
<v Ira Glass>这对每个人来说都不是梦想。为什么那对你来说是梦想？

00:00:45.210 --> 00:00:58.470
<v Jeff>Oh, I just think bears are the coolest animals. They're so cool. Black bears are, like, so cool, how fast they can climb a tree. Like, their ears-- I love their-- bears' ears. I just love everything about bears.
<v Jeff>哦，我只是觉得熊是最酷的动物。它们太酷了。黑熊，真的很酷，它们爬树的速度那么快。还有它们的耳朵——我喜欢它们的——熊耳朵。我喜欢关于熊的一切。

00:00:58.470 --> 00:01:10.320
<v Ira Glass>So Jeff loves bears. His brother, Wes, studies bears. Jeff is the younger brother. Wes is the older brother. Wes's research was to track where bears were in Bryce Canyon National Park in Utah. Here he is.
<v Ira Glass>所以杰夫喜欢熊。他的兄弟韦斯研究熊。杰夫是弟弟，韦斯是哥哥。韦斯的研究是追踪犹他州布莱斯峡谷国家公园里的熊。他就是韦斯。

00:01:10.320 --> 00:01:24.030
<v Wes>There was a bear that had broken into a tent, swatted a woman while she was sleeping, really scared her. And so the idea was to collar some bears, track them, see where they're going, how they're spending their time.
<v Wes>曾经有一只熊闯入帐篷，在一名女子睡觉时拍了她一下，把她吓坏了。所以当时的计划是给一些熊戴上项圈，追踪它们，看看它们去哪，把时间花在哪儿。

00:01:24.030 --> 00:01:45.670
<v Ira Glass>If they could figure that out, the park could relocate campsites away from those areas and post signs. So Wes and Jeff spent a summer putting collars with GPS devices onto a bunch of wild bears. And then in the winter, they had to go back and check on the bears, do a quick exam, make sure their collars weren't too tight once the bears fattened up for winter, swap out any old collars that needed new batteries.
<v Ira Glass>如果他们能搞清楚，公园就能把露营地搬离那些区域并竖起警示牌。于是韦斯和杰夫花了一个夏天给一群野生熊戴上了带有GPS设备的项圈。然后在冬天，他们不得不回去检查那些熊，做一个简单的体检，确保熊在为过冬增肥后项圈没有太紧，并更换那些需要新电池的旧项圈。

00:01:45.670 --> 00:02:00.520
<v Ira Glass>And it turns out the easiest way to do that is to visit the bears when they're hibernating in their dens. In most bear dens, that job isn't actually too difficult or scary. The dens are small, like a cavity in some rock or under a tree, Wes says.
<v Ira Glass>事实证明，最简单的办法就是在熊冬眠时去探望它们。韦斯说，在大多数熊洞里，这项工作其实并不太困难或恐怖。这些洞很小，就像岩石间的空洞或树下的空间。

00:02:00.520 --> 00:02:13.090
<v Wes>And they're generally shallow enough that I can just-- I have a pole that has a syringe on the end of it. I can just extend that pole and sedate the bear from outside of the den. I don't generally even have to go inside.
<v Wes>而且它们通常足够浅，我可以用——我有一根末端带有注射器的杆子。我可以直接伸出那根杆子，在洞外给熊打麻醉剂。通常我甚至不需要进去。

00:02:13.090 --> 00:02:14.410
<v Ira Glass>Generally.
<v Ira Glass>通常情况下。

00:02:14.410 --> 00:02:23.680
<v Jeff>But then, you get a couple dens where it's like you're the only thing between the bear and the exit. And that's when it gets kind of sketchy.
<v Jeff>但后来，你会遇到几个洞，那种情况下，你是熊和出口之间唯一的东西。那时候情况就变得有点危险了。

00:02:23.680 --> 00:02:25.900
<v Ira Glass>And that's what happened in this case.
<v Ira Glass>而这就是这次发生的事情。

00:02:25.900 --> 00:02:37.010
<v Jeff>Oh, this one was, like, on another level, though. This one was crazy.
<v Jeff>哦，不过这一个简直是另一个级别的。这一个太疯狂了。

00:02:37.010 --> 00:02:59.120
<v Ira Glass>This one happened on a wintry day. The two of them headed out to see bear number 95 in their study. They saw a signal on the GPS, climbed maybe 1,000 feet up a steep hillside to get to him in the snow. It was hard and cold. And then they struggled to find any opening at all in the rock that could be a den, because it's just a blanket of snow everywhere. They searched for maybe half an hour.
<v Ira Glass>这次发生在一个冬日。他们两人出发去寻找研究中的95号熊。他们看到GPS信号，在雪地里爬了大约1000英尺陡峭的山坡才找到它。路很难走，天气很冷。然后他们费力地寻找任何可能是洞穴的岩石开口，因为到处都是厚厚的积雪。他们找了大概半个小时。

00:02:59.120 --> 00:03:17.690
<v Jeff>And then Wes is, like, standing on this, like, tiny little opening. And then I see these branches right by his feet. And a lot of times, bears will, like, bring branches into their den. So I'm like, Wes, like, I think you're standing on top of the den. (CHUCKLING) And he's just like, no way.
<v Jeff>然后韦斯就站在一个——一个小小的开口上。我看到他脚边有树枝。很多时候，熊会把树枝带进洞里。所以我说，韦斯，我觉得你正站在洞口上。（轻笑）而他说，不可能。

00:03:17.690 --> 00:03:23.780
<v Ira Glass>They clear away the snow, and it's an opening the size of a manhole cover, going into the side of a sandstone cliff.
<v Ira Glass>他们扫开雪，那是一个井盖大小的洞口，通往砂岩悬崖的一侧。

00:03:23.780 --> 00:03:52.360
<v Jeff>And then we crouched down and looked. And, like-- I mean, you could only see, like, 20 feet in, and it's just black. So honestly, this den-- it would be terrifying to go in it if you, like, didn't know anything was in there. And you know that there's a bear in there, so that just intensifies it so much more. But it's like even if there wasn't a bear, I'd be like, this is scary.
<v Jeff>然后我们蹲下看了看。我的意思是，你只能看进去20英尺，里面是一片漆黑。说实话，如果你不知道里面有什么，进去这个洞会很恐怖。你知道里面有一只熊，那只会让这种恐怖加剧。但即使没有熊，我也会觉得，这太吓人了。

00:03:52.360 --> 00:04:07.030
<v Ira Glass>Wes and Jeff discuss whether they're going to go into this hole. Black bears aren't like grizzlies. They almost never attack people. But going into this very unusual den where the bear might feel trapped or threatened and have no way out except through them-- that's a choice.
<v Ira Glass>韦斯和杰夫讨论是否要进这个洞。黑熊不像灰熊。它们几乎从不攻击人。但进入这个非常不寻常的洞穴，熊可能会感到被困或受到威胁，除了经过他们之外别无去路——这是一个选择。

00:04:07.030 --> 00:04:17.980
<v Ira Glass>Jeff does talk back to Wes way more than any normal field tech would, because he's his brother. But Wes is the boss, and he's like, we came this far, and we really do need to change that bear's collar.
<v Ira Glass>杰夫回嘴韦斯的频率确实比任何普通的技术员都要高，因为他是他弟弟。但韦斯是老板，他说，我们都到这儿了，我们确实需要更换那只熊的项圈。

00:04:17.980 --> 00:04:37.880
<v Wes>And I start crawling in, looking for the back of the den, looking for the bear, and it's just not there. And this hole just plunges into darkness. My headlamp only goes so far. And I just keep going. And immediately, I realize, like, shit, this is a really, really long den. This is much longer than anything I've ever seen or even heard of.
<v Wes>我开始爬进去，寻找洞穴的深处，寻找那只熊，但它不在那儿。这个洞不断深入黑暗。我的头灯照不了多远。我继续前进。我立刻意识到，糟了，这是一个非常、非常长的洞。这比我见过甚至听说过的任何洞都要长。

00:04:37.880 --> 00:04:39.320
<v Ira Glass>How long?
<v Ira Glass>有多长？

00:04:39.320 --> 00:04:54.200
<v Wes>This thing extends 80 feet back, and at the end of it, it's just pitch black. You kind of-- you really do have to suppress a lot of primal stuff that's telling you this is a place where you are not supposed to be. This is not a place for humans.
<v Wes>这个东西向后延伸了80英尺，尽头是一片漆黑。你确实需要抑制住很多原始的本能，它们在告诉你，这地方不是你该来的。这不是人类该待的地方。

00:04:54.200 --> 00:05:04.070
<v Ira Glass>Wes is in front, big brother, scientist. Jeff's behind-- little brother, assistant. And they're army-crawling, because the tunnel is too low to stand up in. It's pretty cramped.
<v Ira Glass>韦斯在前面，哥哥，科学家。杰夫在后面——弟弟，助理。他们采取匍匐前进的姿势，因为隧道太低无法站立。里面很挤。

00:05:04.070 --> 00:05:13.820
<v Ira Glass>And you know that feeling where you're in your belly in a tunnel, inching towards a bear? No, of course not. You don't know that feeling. Who has done that?
<v Ira Glass>你明白那种腹部贴地在隧道里向着熊挪动的感觉吗？不，当然不。你不知道那种感觉。谁做过那种事？

00:05:13.820 --> 00:05:32.960
<v Ira Glass>But you have been in this situation of moving toward something scary or dreadful ahead. And now, you have gotten yourself in it. You can't turn back. You have to keep moving. You just have to deal. And that is what today's show is about. We have this story, and then we have another one in the second half of the show. And yes, it's This American Life from WBEZ Chicago, Ira Glass.
<v Ira Glass>但你肯定有过走向恐惧或可怕之物的情况。现在，你把自己置于其中了。你不能回头。你必须继续前进。你只能去应对。这就是今天节目的主题。我们有这个故事，节目下半场还有另一个故事。没错，这是来自芝加哥WBEZ的《美国生活》，我是艾拉·格拉斯。

00:05:32.960 --> 00:05:43.400
<v Ira Glass>Anyway, so they're edging towards this bear, right? The tunnel was gently sloping downward. And the smell-- you may be wondering about the smell. It's like wet earth, musty.
<v Ira Glass>总之，他们正在向那只熊靠近，对吧？隧道向下倾斜。至于气味——你可能好奇气味。就像湿润的泥土，带着霉味。

00:05:43.400 --> 00:05:50.420
<v Wes>The bear brings in a nest of, like, pine tree boughs and other sticks and stuff. So it does kind of smell cedary and nice, too.
<v Wes>熊会带进一个由松枝和其他树枝之类的东西筑成的窝。所以它闻起来也有点像雪松味，还不错。

00:05:50.420 --> 00:05:51.530
<v Ira Glass>Hmm.
<v Ira Glass>嗯。

00:05:51.530 --> 00:06:17.160
<v Jeff>Like, it's musty, but it's honestly not as bad as what you would think. Because when a bear goes into hibernation-- Wes will give you a lot more scientific name on this, but they create, like, a butt plug of, like, poop, so that they won't poop the entire time they're in their den. So there's, like, no feces or urine in there. So it honestly isn't terrible.
<v Jeff>闻起来发霉，但老实说，没你想的那么糟糕。因为当熊进入冬眠时——韦斯会给你更多专业名词——它们会制造一个像排泄物塞子一样的东西，这样它们在洞里的时候就不会排便。所以里面没有粪便或尿液。所以坦白讲，并不难闻。

00:06:17.160 --> 00:06:22.680
<v Ira Glass>They may be 15, 20, 25 feet into the tunnel, and Wes's headlamp points at the far end.
<v Ira Glass>他们可能已经进入隧道25英尺了，韦斯的头灯照向了最深处。

00:06:22.680 --> 00:06:39.510
<v Wes>And I just see these two green eyes at the very end of this tunnel, staring back at me. And the thing about these hibernating bears is they-- bears don't hibernate very deeply. So if a bear hears you approaching its den, it can come out of that hibernation, and generally, within 5 to 10 minutes, they can be mobile.
<v Wes>我看到隧道尽头有两只绿色的眼睛，盯着我看。关于这些冬眠的熊，它们——熊的冬眠并不非常深。所以如果熊听到你靠近它的洞穴，它就能从冬眠中醒来，通常在5到10分钟内，它们就能活动了。

00:06:39.510 --> 00:07:01.830
<v Wes>And so this bear was awake. And they have the tapetum lucidum, which is the thing you see in cats' eyes or deer eyes or whatever that reflects light back at you. And they reflect a really kind of green glow. And so it's just, again, the stuff of nightmares. Like, you're crawling down a deep, dark hole in the mountains, and there's two green eyes shining back at you.
<v Wes>所以这只熊醒着。它们有照膜，就是你在猫眼或鹿眼之类看到的反射光的东西。它们反射出一种非常绿色的光。所以，这又是噩梦般的场景。你在山里爬进一个又深又黑的洞，那里有两只绿眼睛在盯着你。

00:07:01.830 --> 00:07:20.160
<v Jeff>The light starts to catch these two eyeballs at the very end of the den that are just, like, gleaming back at us. So at that point, I'm, like, terrified. And I start kind of freaking out. I start saying, Wes, this is crazy, this is crazy. Like, I don't know about this.
<v Jeff>光线开始照到洞穴尽头那两颗正对着我们闪闪发光的眼珠。那一刻，我很恐慌。我开始有点崩溃。我开始说，韦斯，这太疯狂了，这太疯狂了。我不知道该怎么办。

00:07:20.160 --> 00:07:25.600
<v Wes>And Jeff just started saying, we shouldn't be here, we shouldn't be here, we shouldn't be here. It was like a mantra that he was repeating.
<v Wes>杰夫开始说，我们不该在这儿，我们不该在这儿，我们不该在这儿。就像他在重复一段咒语。

00:07:25.600 --> 00:07:27.490
<v Jeff>And, like, I keep saying that.
<v Jeff>而且，我一直在重复那句话。

00:07:27.490 --> 00:07:40.570
<v Wes>And it was starting to really freak me out. Like, I was trying to summon all this courage and draw on all my experience, and then I have him behind me telling me that we just should not be in there. And so I turned around.
<v Wes>这开始让我真的感到恐慌。我试图鼓起所有的勇气，利用我所有的经验，但他却在我身后告诉我，我们根本不该待在那儿。所以我转过身。

00:07:40.570 --> 00:07:50.710
<v Jeff>And then Wes just goes, Jeff, shut up. [CHUCKLES] So then my immediate response was like, you're so brave. [CHUCKLES]
<v Jeff>然后韦斯说，杰夫，闭嘴。（轻笑）我当时的第一反应是，你太勇敢了。（轻笑）

00:07:50.710 --> 00:08:00.440
<v Wes>And then, he just started repeating, you're the bravest person I've ever met, you're the bravest person I've ever met. And that helped, actually. That made me feel a lot better.
<v Wes>然后，他开始重复，你是见过最勇敢的人，你是见过最勇敢的人。这确实有帮助。这让我感觉好多了。

00:08:00.440 --> 00:08:05.230
<v Ira Glass>It's funny how he needs to keep saying something to have some place to put his fear.
<v Ira Glass>真有趣，他需要不断说点什么，好给自己找个地方安放恐惧。

00:08:05.230 --> 00:08:21.580
<v Wes>Yeah. Yeah. I think, for him, he needed to vocalize that, and for me, I was really going inward and just trying to focus on the bear. And I was looking for any cues that that bear was going to be aggressive. And at that point, you leave.
<v Wes>是的。是的。我想，对他来说，他需要把那些话说出来，而对我来说，我确实在向内收敛，试着专注于那只熊。我在寻找任何那只熊要表现出攻击性的迹象。如果那样，你就得离开。

00:08:21.580 --> 00:08:24.370
<v Ira Glass>What are you watching for?
<v Ira Glass>你在观察什么？

00:08:24.370 --> 00:08:39.910
<v Wes>They make a really distinctive [HUFFING] sound when they're really, really upset. And then, they also can-- they make this really interesting jaw-popping noise, too. It literally clacks its jaws. It pops its jaws.
<v Wes>当它们非常、非常愤怒时，会发出一种非常明显的（喷气）声。然后，它们还可以——它们会发出这种非常有趣的颌骨碰撞声。它们真的会磕碰颌骨。

00:08:39.910 --> 00:08:56.170
<v Wes>It's kind of like a [CLICKING] sound. And then from there, it proceeds to swatting the ground with its paws. And then if it is still upset, it'll bluff-charge you. And a bluff charge is just where the bear comes at you, and then stops short.
<v Wes>就像一种（咔哒）声。然后在此基础上，它会开始用爪子拍打地面。如果它依然愤怒，它会虚张声势地冲向你。虚张声势的冲锋就是熊冲向你，然后在最后停下。

00:08:56.170 --> 00:08:58.450
<v Ira Glass>Are you terrified?
<v Ira Glass>你感到恐怖吗？

00:08:58.450 --> 00:09:10.020
<v Wes>Oh, yeah. Oh, yeah, I was absolutely terrified. I have a video that I took when I got close to the bear. I took a quick video from my phone. And at the very end of it, you just hear me say, this is so scary.
<v Wes>哦，是的。哦，是的，我绝对感到恐怖。我接近那只熊时拍过一个视频。我用手机拍了个短视频。视频最后，你能听到我说，这太吓人了。

00:09:10.020 --> 00:09:10.930
<v Wes>[AUDIO PLAYBACK]
<v Wes>（音频播放）

00:09:10.930 --> 00:09:16.780
<v Wes>(OUT OF BREATH) OK. Oh, this is so fucking scary. This is so scary.
<v Wes>（上气不接下气）好吧。哦，这他妈太吓人了。这太吓人了。

00:09:16.780 --> 00:09:17.200
<v Wes>[END PLAYBACK]
<v Wes>（播放结束）

00:09:17.200 --> 00:09:19.330
<v Wes>Because it really was. It was terrifying.
<v Wes>因为确实如此。这太恐怖了。

00:09:19.330 --> 00:09:19.750
<v Wes>[AUDIO PLAYBACK]
<v Wes>（音频播放）

00:09:19.750 --> 00:09:22.860
<v Wes>[PANTING] Oh my gosh.
<v Wes>（喘气）我的天啊。

00:09:22.860 --> 00:09:23.200
<v Wes>[END PLAYBACK]
<v Wes>（播放结束）

00:09:23.200 --> 00:09:43.720
<v Wes>All of my body, a lot of my psyche was saying, you absolutely need to get out of this den. It's this feeling of being a soft, pink human being in a place where we don't belong.
<v Wes>我的整个身体、大部分心理都在说，你绝对需要离开这个洞。这是一种身为软弱的粉红色人类，置身于不属于我们地方的感觉。

00:09:43.720 --> 00:09:52.330
<v Ira Glass>Of course, Wes, being in the tunnel with this bear-- he's a bear expert. And he's thought some about what this experience must have been like for the bear.
<v Ira Glass>当然，作为在隧道里和熊在一起的人——韦斯是熊类专家。他想过这种经历对熊来说会是什么样。

00:09:52.330 --> 00:10:02.550
<v Wes>Where these black bears live, they're top dog. There's nothing bigger or more dangerous than them. And so they don't have stuff that crawls into their dens and gets closer and closer to them.
<v Wes>在这些黑熊生活的地方，它们是老大。没有比它们更大或更危险的了。所以它们没有会爬进洞穴、离它们越来越近的东西。

00:10:02.550 --> 00:10:11.300
<v Wes>So it must be a very unique experience for them as well. Like, I'm obviously a bear. Like, what the hell is this thing doing in here?
<v Wes>所以对它们来说，这肯定也是一种非常独特的体验。就像，我显然是一只熊。到底是什么东西在这儿？

00:10:11.300 --> 00:10:26.390
<v Ira Glass>Wait, when the bear sees you, like, it knows it can kick your ass, right? Like, it can just look at you, and it doesn't even-- without even thinking or doing any math in its head or something, it just sees your size, and it just knows, well, you're not a threat. But then, is it scared?
<v Ira Glass>等等，当熊看到你时，它知道它能轻松干掉你，对吧？它只需要看着你，甚至不用思考，不用在大脑里做算术，它看到你的体型，就知道你不是威胁。但它害怕吗？

00:10:26.390 --> 00:10:59.200
<v Wes>Yeah, you know, I think the thing that we have to realize with wild animals is that for them, a fight means much more than can I win or will I lose. It's like, am I going to be injured in this? Because for any animal, bears included, if they sustain a big injury in any kind of scuffle or fight or whatever, that could be a death sentence for them. Because then they no longer have the ability to feed or escape or even move. And so they tend to be very risk-averse.
<v Wes>是的，你知道，我认为我们需要意识到的是，对于野生动物来说，战斗意味着比“我赢还是输”多得多。意思是，我会在其中受伤吗？因为对任何动物，包括熊，如果它们在任何斗殴或争斗中受了重伤，那对它们来说可能就是死刑判决。因为那样它们就失去了进食、逃跑甚至移动的能力。所以它们往往非常规避风险。

00:10:59.200 --> 00:11:11.110
<v Ira Glass>So Wes and Jeff keep army-crawling forward with a syringe full of sedative on the end of a 7-foot pole. And finally, Wes gets close enough to jab it into the bear. Mission accomplished, right?
<v Ira Glass>所以韦斯和杰夫继续匍匐前进，拿着一根7英尺长的杆子，顶端装有装满镇静剂的注射器。最后，韦斯靠得足够近，给熊打了一针。任务完成，对吧？

00:11:11.110 --> 00:11:15.220
<v Jeff>But we jab it. The bear doesn't react at all.
<v Jeff>但我们刺进去了。熊一点反应都没有。

00:11:15.220 --> 00:11:35.440
<v Ira Glass>Doesn't flinch, and most important, does not fall asleep, partly because it'd gotten so big since they'd last seen it-- like, 350 pounds-- which would require way more sedative than West gave it. So the two of them head back out of the cave to prepare another dose of sedative, which they definitely are going to need. That job goes to Jeff, the little brother.
<v Ira Glass>没退缩，最重要的是，没有睡着。部分原因在于它自上次见到后长得太大了——大约350磅——这需要的镇静剂比韦斯给它的要多得多。所以他们两人折回洞外准备第二剂镇静剂，这是他们肯定会需要的。这项工作交给了弟弟杰夫。

00:11:35.440 --> 00:11:49.540
<v Jeff>And it was something I was fully capable of doing, but I think I was still just dialed up all the way to 11, with all my emotions, and my thoughts weren't clear. So I was doing a terrible job.
<v Jeff>这本是我完全能做到的事情，但我认为当时我的情绪依然处于极度紧张的状态，思绪不够清晰。所以我做得很糟。

00:11:49.540 --> 00:11:51.670
<v Ira Glass>Like your hands aren't totally steady?
<v Ira Glass>就像你的手完全不稳？

00:11:51.670 --> 00:12:03.440
<v Jeff>Yeah, I'm just making a mess. I'm, like, fumbling around. And eventually, the bear, like, makes its way all the way through the 80 feet of den and starts coming out.
<v Jeff>是的，我把事情弄得一团糟。我在笨手笨脚地忙活。最终，那只熊沿着80英尺长的洞穴往外走，开始出来了。

00:12:03.440 --> 00:12:21.080
<v Ira Glass>This is not the plan. It would be very bad for a tranq'd-out bear to leave its den at all. It could easily wander off and get lost in the snow in the middle of winter. And Wes and Jeff-- remember, they're bear guys. They do anything they can think of so the bears in this research don't get hurt by it, and experience the minimum amount of discomfort or stress.
<v Ira Glass>这不是计划。如果一只被打了麻醉的熊离开洞穴，那将非常糟糕。它很容易走失，在冬天冻死在雪地里。而韦斯和杰夫——记住，他们是熊专家。他们会想尽一切办法，不让研究中的熊受到伤害，并使它们经历最低程度的不适或压力。

00:12:21.080 --> 00:12:32.120
<v Ira Glass>They have another field tech with them who's been waiting outside the tunnel. And Wes orders that guy now, keep the bear in the den. And the guy starts swatting at the bear with a plastic shovel to keep it from emerging.
<v Ira Glass>他们身边还有另一位外勤技术员，一直在隧道外等待。韦斯命令那个人，现在，把熊挡在洞里。那人开始用塑料铲子拍打熊，以阻止它出来。

00:12:32.120 --> 00:12:38.360
<v Wes>And it just does not care. It's not even registering that he's there and just keeps walking past him.
<v Wes>但它完全不在乎。它甚至没察觉到他在那儿，径直从他身边走了过去。

00:12:38.360 --> 00:12:38.930
<v Ira Glass>Wow.
<v Ira Glass>哇。

00:12:38.930 --> 00:12:56.760
<v Wes>So yeah. So at that point, I ran. And the thing that I really didn't want to have happen was the drug take hold while this bear was trying to get away and have it tumble down the hillside or down a cliffside or something like that. I just wasn't willing to let that happen.
<v Wes>所以，是的。在那一点上，我跑过去了。我最不想发生的事情就是药物在熊试图逃跑时起效，导致它滚下山坡或悬崖什么的。我绝不允许这种情况发生。

00:12:56.760 --> 00:13:08.350
<v Wes>So I ran and actually grabbed the bear by its back legs and pulled them out from under it. And then I straddled the bear and pushed its head down into the snow. In the meantime, I had sent--
<v Wes>所以我跑过去，真的抓住了熊的后腿，把它从下面拽了出来。然后我骑在熊背上，把它的头按进雪里。同时，我派——

00:13:08.350 --> 00:13:20.480
<v Ira Glass>Wait, wait, wait, wait. What are you-- what are you talking about? You're able to do that because the bear is kind of like-- is the bear walking like a sort of cartoon drunk bear kind of thing? Like, it's staggering along?
<v Ira Glass>等等，等等，等等。你在说什——你在说什么？你能做到这一点是因为那只熊有点像——那只熊走起来像卡通里的醉熊吗？踉踉跄跄的？

00:13:20.480 --> 00:13:24.500
<v Wes>Yeah, that's honestly exactly how it was walking. That's a perfect representation.
<v Wes>是的，老实说，它走起来就是那样。那是一个完美的比喻。

00:13:24.500 --> 00:13:26.130
<v Wes>[CHUCKLING]
<v Wes>（轻笑）

00:13:26.130 --> 00:13:41.960
<v Ira Glass>So he's on top of this bear, straddling it, holding its collar and head down in the snow. He's actually gotten a second dose of sedative into him by then, but the bear is still awake. And Wes then looks over at Jeff, who is so panicked that he has dropped the entire case of drugs. Jeff is supposed to be preparing a third dose.
<v Ira Glass>所以他骑在熊背上，跨坐在它上面，按着它的项圈和头部埋在雪里。他当时已经给它打了第二剂镇静剂，但熊还清醒着。韦斯看了一眼杰夫，杰夫已经吓坏了，把整盒药都扔了。杰夫本该准备第三剂的。

00:13:41.960 --> 00:13:47.660
<v Wes>And I just yell at Jeff. I say, Jeff, come grab this bear. I'll do the drugs.
<v Wes>我冲杰夫大喊。我说，杰夫，快来抓住这只熊。我来处理药。

00:13:47.660 --> 00:13:50.330
<v Ira Glass>Kind of an unusual set of sentences to say to anybody.
<v Ira Glass>这对他说的这些话还挺不寻常的。

00:13:50.330 --> 00:13:58.740
<v Ira Glass>They switch places. Jeff is now the one straddling the bear, hands on its collar. And the bear gets up and starts to move again.
<v Ira Glass>他们交换了位置。现在杰夫跨坐在熊身上，手抓着它的项圈。熊站起来开始又动了。

