<v Jeff>And it's almost like-- you know that movie, The Replacements, where Keanu Reeves is a quarterback? Or any of the football movies where the big hefty lineman catches the ball?
<v Jeff>这几乎就像——你知道那部电影《替补球员》吗，基努·里维斯是四分卫？或者是任何橄榄球电影里，体型巨大的前锋接住了球？

00:14:10.810 --> 00:14:11.390
<v Ira Glass>Mm-hmm.
<v Ira Glass>嗯。

00:14:11.390 --> 00:14:20.240
<v Jeff>And then they're just, like, slowly moving towards the end zone, and the whole defense jumps on him, and they're just kind of, like, falling off of him and trucking...
<v Jeff>然后他们就那样慢慢地向达阵区移动，整个防守组都跳到他身上，他们只是那种，从他身上掉下来，然后拖着……

00:14:20.240 --> 00:14:22.850
<v Ira Glass>And he's still going towards the goal line? Yeah,
<v Ira Glass>而他还在向球门线推进？是的，

00:14:22.850 --> 00:14:33.620
<v Jeff>...is going in a straight line with all these people hanging on him? It's kind of like that. We were just hanging on this black bear, and it's just, like, slowly going in a straight line.
<v Jeff>……在所有这些人挂在他身上的情况下，直线前进？有点像那样。我们只是挂在那只黑熊身上，而它就像，慢慢地走直线。

00:14:33.620 --> 00:14:43.730
<v Ira Glass>Jeff's holding it by the collar, getting dragged along-- slowing it down a little bit, but that's all he can do, in this thing with this bear where none of the normal rules apply, totally off the map.
<v Ira Glass>杰夫抓着项圈，被拖着走——稍微减慢了它的速度，但他所能做的也只有这些，在这样一个常规规则完全不适用的地方，完全处于地图之外。

00:14:43.730 --> 00:14:56.700
<v Jeff>I was like, this is what I have to do, but I can't believe I'm doing it. It was, like, surreal. Like, I just-- it's like I was in a dream.
<v Jeff>我觉得，这是我必须做的，但我无法相信我在做这事。这很超现实。就像，我只是——就像我身处梦境。

00:14:56.700 --> 00:15:20.850
<v Jeff>It starts making its way to more of a downward slope, too, so now, it's really hard for me to stop it. And I'm just kind of, like, on the back of it. And it walks straight into a pine tree, and literally, like, couldn't figure out what was going on and just tried to keep walking forward into a tree.
<v Jeff>它开始向着更陡的下坡路走，所以现在我很难阻止它了。我有点像，在它背上。它直直地撞进一棵松树里，老实说，它完全没搞清楚发生了什么，只是试图继续向前走进树里。

00:15:20.850 --> 00:15:31.500
<v Ira Glass>Wes remembers it more like a juniper bush, but in any case, it does the job. The bear stops, finally passes out on the ground. They do their business, change the collar, examine the bear.
<v Ira Glass>韦斯记得那更像是一丛杜松，但无论如何，这起了作用。熊停下来了，终于倒在地上昏睡过去。他们处理完工作，换上项圈，检查了熊。

00:15:31.500 --> 00:15:45.960
<v Ira Glass>And then, so the bear doesn't get lost and confused in the snow when it wakes up, they grab its limbs and drag it 15 yards or so back up the slope to the opening of the tunnel, which is exactly as completely exhausting as you would imagine. It takes maybe half an hour.
<v Ira Glass>然后，为了让熊醒来时不会在雪地里迷路和困惑，他们抓住它的四肢，把它拖回坡上大约15码远的地方，带回洞穴入口，这和你想象的一样耗尽体力。大概花了半个小时。

00:15:45.960 --> 00:15:58.590
<v Ira Glass>The temperature is dropping. Wes is worried that the bear might get cold, so he leaves a blanket on top of him before they go.
<v Ira Glass>温度在下降。韦斯担心熊可能会冷，所以他在离开前在它身上盖了一条毯子。

00:15:58.590 --> 00:16:13.500
<v Ira Glass>Looking back, what Wes and Jeff both talk about is the terror that they felt in that tunnel. Wes says it was a whole new level of fear he didn't know existed, like the kind of primal fear that, long ago, humans must have had all the time facing off with animals that wanted to kill them and eat them.
<v Ira Glass>回顾过去，韦斯和杰夫谈论的都是他们在隧道里感受到的恐惧。韦斯说那是他不知道存在的一种全新的恐惧水平，就像很久以前人类在面对想要杀死并吃掉它们的动物时，肯定一直存在的那种原始恐惧。

00:16:13.500 --> 00:17:03.340
<v Wes>And it's something that we've forgotten, but I think it's something that still sits deep inside of us, that when you do have these experiences with large animals, that it bubbles to the surface. And in a way, it was really intoxicating. I'll tell you, that night, I didn't sleep a wink, because my adrenaline was just coursing through my body. It just made me feel-- honestly, made me feel very alive. And even when I think back on that, I can still remember the feelings I had that night as I went back to my cabin and everything, just how bright the stars seemed and how crisp the air felt. And it really just reminded me how good it is to be alive.
<v Wes>这是一种我们已经忘记的东西，但我认为它仍然深深地藏在我们内心深处，当你与大型动物有这些经历时，它就会浮出水面。某种程度上，这真的很让人着迷。我告诉你，那天晚上我一夜没睡，因为肾上腺素在我体内奔涌。它让我感觉——老实说，让我感觉非常活着。即使现在回想起那天，我依然记得那天晚上回到小屋时的感受，星星显得多么明亮，空气显得多么清新。它真的提醒了我活着是多么美好。

00:17:03.340 --> 00:17:38.450
<v Ira Glass>And the bear? The bear was fine. The drugs they gave it, it probably doesn't remember the details of what happened, Wes said. And over a year later, Wes went back to that tunnel when the bear wasn't in it and found the blanket inside. The bear must have liked it a little. It smelled like bear, fur all over it. Wes took it back, kept it as a souvenir. He says it still smells faintly like bear.
<v Ira Glass>那只熊呢？熊没事。韦斯说，给它的药让它可能记不清发生了什么。一年多后，韦斯回到那个熊不在的洞穴，发现了里面的毯子。熊一定有点喜欢它。上面满是熊毛味。韦斯把它带了回去，当成纪念品留着。他说它现在还有淡淡的熊味。

00:17:38.450 --> 00:17:52.940
<v Ira Glass>After the break, we have another story of people thrown into a dangerous and utterly foreseeable situation, and how they handle it, in this episode that we're calling "The Bear at the End of the Tunnel." Wes and Jeff, by the way, have a podcast where they tell stories of animal attacks. It's called Tooth and Claw.
<v Ira Glass>广告回来后，我们还有另一个关于人们被迫陷入危险且完全可预见的境地，以及他们如何应对的故事，这集我们称为“隧道尽头的熊”。顺便说一句，韦斯和杰夫有一个讲述动物攻击故事的播客。叫《牙齿与爪子》。

00:17:52.940 --> 00:18:07.750
<v Ira Glass>If you enjoyed this story that you just heard, you should know that by their standards, this story was not so special. The ones on their show-- way more eventful. More in a minute from Chicago Public Radio when our program continues.
